#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082924 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "345d6df7-2ada-5c2e-8146-7c779afae3af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/id #uuid "e930a29a-6adf-5aaa-9b44-86dd8d570ae7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5354" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola\n$4 y^2 - 10 x^2 - 8 y - 40 x = 76$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4y^{2} - 10x^{2} - 8y - 40x &= 76 \\\\\n4y^{2} - 8y - 10x^{2} - 40x &= 76 \\\\\n4[y^{2} - 2y] - 10[x^{2} + 4x] &= 76 \\\\\n4[(y - 1)^{2} - 1] - 10[(x + 2)^{2} - 4] &= 76 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 4 - 10(x + 2)^{2} + 40 &= 76 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 10(x + 2)^{2} &= 76 - 40 + 4 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 10(x + 2)^{2} &= 40 \\\\\n((y - 1)^{2})/(10) - ((x + 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(-2,1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082991 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f99d4fec-bc58-5a09-9fac-38b46f233489" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/id #uuid "0caa1041-a564-5428-9838-20cea32fdf17" :question/math-academy-id "e-6775" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9} - \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{4} = 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h = 1$ and $k = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(1,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082992 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdab0134-e416-51cf-89fa-73ad4a2c2eb9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/id #uuid "8e31f5b5-ff87-5c45-b143-66dfdcb4ab8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6776" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(y+3)^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9} = 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-(-3))^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h = 1$ and $k=-3$.\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(1,-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "223e29c9-1bb5-51ee-9ec8-4d5c15363951" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/id #uuid "af591507-e248-53d2-a391-e053cdc3fdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-6777" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola\n$x^2 - 4 y^2- 2 x + 16 y = 31$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4y^{2} - 2x + 16y &= 31 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 4y^{2} + 16y &= 31 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 4[y^{2} - 4y] &= 31 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 1 - 4[(y - 2)^{2} - 4] &= 31 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 1 - 4(y - 2)^{2} + 16 &= 31 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 4(y - 2)^{2} &= 31 + 1 - 16 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 4(y - 2)^{2} &= 16 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(16) - ((y - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(1,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "223e29c9-1bb5-51ee-9ec8-4d5c15363951" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :que